        "student_prefix_sha256": "2c9be149aa4abf289cda8e02a484c7be3acb9a1e0203972c24b70c90ec743824",
        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities: the total capacity of the library (400 books), the current number of books (120 books), and the desired fullness (90%). The unknown I needed to find was how many more books Karson needs to buy to reach 90% of the library's capacity.\n\nFirst, I calculated 90% of the library's total capacity:\n\\[ 0.90 \\times 400 = 360 \\text{ books} \\]\nThis step was necessary to determine the target number of books for 90% fullness.\n\nNext, I subtracted the current number of books from the target number to find out how many more books are needed:\n\\[ 360 - 120 = 240 \\text{ books} \\]\nThis subtraction operation directly gave me the number of books Karson needs to buy.\n\nTo verify the result, I checked if adding 240 books to the current 120 books would indeed make the library 90% full:\n\\[ 120 + 240 = 360 \\text{ books} \\]\nSince 360 books is 90% of the library's capacity (as calculated earlier), the result is correct.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is the subtraction of the current number of books from the target number of books to find the number of books needed to reach a specific percentage of capacity. This operation is reusable whenever a similar problem involving a target percentage and current quantity needs to be solved.\n\nFINAL: 240",
